{{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     theme-init — MUST stay inline, blocking, and near the top of <head>.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Adapted from NS-Design-System's assets/js/theme-init.js, which says at the
     top of the file that it has to be inlined rather than linked.

     THE PROBLEM. The page paints once, before any deferred script runs. Apply
     the theme after that and a dark-mode reader gets a white flash on every
     single navigation. It has exactly one fix: set the attribute
     synchronously, in the head, before first paint. A <script src> is fetched
     separately and lands too late — no amount of defer/async tuning helps.

     WHY THE REASONING IS OUT HERE. Everything below ships to every reader on
     every page view, so the explanation lives in this Handlebars comment,
     which is stripped at render. Spelling the comment-close sequence out
     inside a comment would end the comment early and dump the rest into the
     page as visible text — a leaked comment is valid HTML, so nothing catches
     it. That has already happened once while writing this theme.

     WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: decide what dark mode looks like. That is entirely
     NSDS's tokens, which flip on [data-theme="dark"]. This script only records
     an explicit CHOICE, which CSS has no way to store.

     THE PARTS THAT ARE NOT OBVIOUS:

     · localStorage is read inside try/catch because Safari in private mode and
       some embedded webviews THROW on access rather than returning null. A
       theme script must never be the thing that breaks the page.

     · "System" is stored as the ABSENCE of a value, not the string "system".
       That is what lets a reader who has never chosen keep following their OS,
       and what makes useSystem() a removeItem call.

     · root.style.colorScheme is set alongside the attribute so form controls,
       scrollbars and the canvas render dark in the first frame too. Without it
       a dark page still gets a white scrollbar on load.

     · window.nsTheme is the NSDS name and the storage key is NSDS's, both kept
       deliberately: assets/js/nav.js is vendored from NSDS unchanged and calls
       it, and a shared key means a reader moving between this site and the LMS
       does not get a different theme on each.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
<script>
(function(){"use strict";
var KEY="ns-theme",root=document.documentElement;
function os(){return window.matchMedia&&window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches?"dark":"light";}
function read(){try{var v=localStorage.getItem(KEY);return v==="light"||v==="dark"?v:null;}catch(e){return null;}}
function apply(t){root.setAttribute("data-theme",t);root.style.colorScheme=t;}
apply(read()||os());
if(window.matchMedia){
  var mq=window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)"),on=function(e){
    if(read())return;
    apply(e.matches?"dark":"light");
  };
  if(mq.addEventListener)mq.addEventListener("change",on);else if(mq.addListener)mq.addListener(on);
}
window.nsTheme={
  key:KEY,
  get:function(){return root.getAttribute("data-theme");},
  set:function(v){apply(v);try{localStorage.setItem(KEY,v);}catch(e){}},
  toggle:function(){this.set(root.getAttribute("data-theme")==="dark"?"light":"dark");},
  useSystem:function(){try{localStorage.removeItem(KEY);}catch(e){}apply(os());}
};
})();
</script>
